From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: athexweb3 <athexweb3@nirapod.org>
Date: Sun, 18 Oct 2026 13:10:31 +0600
Subject: [PATCH] armv8a: add the basic Advanced SIMD instructions

With the vector registers in place, u128 values can be moved but not
computed on. AND, BIC, ORR, EOR, NOT, ADD, SUB, SHL, USHR, SSHR, SRI,
SLI and MOVI are added as intrinsics, and the u128 operators are
lowered to them: the bitwise operators, element-wise addition and
subtraction, shifts by a constant, and the constant zero. An
instruction that exists at several element sizes takes its arrangement
as a suffix, as on x86: #VADD_16u8 is ADD Vd.16B.

BIC is chosen through the shared is_andn, so both a & !b and !b & a
become one BIC, as for the general-purpose registers. A shift is
lowered only when its amount fits the immediate form: 0 to esize - 1
for SHL, and 1 to esize for USHR and SSHR, where shifting by esize
gives what the Jasmin operator gives. SHL and SLI check their amount
with the existing shift-amount condition, now used at every element
size, and USHR, SSHR and SRI with a new one for 1 to esize. MOVI only
takes #0, the one constant that is lowered.

MOVI isn't a data-independent-time instruction (DDI 0487 M.d, C5.2.4
doesn't list it). That's harmless here, since its operand is a
constant. The other instructions are all data-independent-time.

There are no shifts by a register amount, no constant vectors other
than zero, and no multiplication or comparison. Any other u128
operator is still a compile error.

Co-authored-by: orvenweb3 <orvenweb3@gmail.com>
